## Who Owns the Value?

**Ownership** is XIOM's core memory management mechanism. Every value has exactly one owner at any time. When the owner goes out of scope, the value is destroyed.

### The Three Ownership Rules

1. **Each value has one owner** — the variable that holds it
2. **Only one owner at a time** — no shared ownership by default
3. **When the owner leaves scope, the value is dropped** — memory freed, resources closed

### Ownership in Action

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let s = String.new("hello");  // s owns the string
  // ... use s ...
  return 0;
  // s goes out of scope here — string is freed
}
```

### Ownership Transfer

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let a = Vec[Int]();
  a.push(1);
  let b = a;  // OWNERSHIP MOVES from a to b
  // a is now invalid — cannot use a anymore
  b.push(2);  // b is the owner
  return b.len();
  // b goes out of scope — Vec freed
}
```

### Ownership and Functions

```xiom
fn take_ownership(v: Vec[Int]) -> Int {
  let len = v.len();
  // v goes out of scope — Vec freed
  return len;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let data = Vec[Int]();
  data.push(1);
  let len = take_ownership(data);  // data MOVED into function
  // data is no longer valid here
  return len;
}
```

### Why Ownership?

- **No garbage collector pauses** — memory freed at predictable points
- **No double-free bugs** — compiler proves only one owner exists
- **No use-after-free** — compiler rejects use of moved values
- **RAII pattern** — resource acquisition is initialization, cleanup is deterministic

